__d(function(e,t,i,o,a,n,r){a.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:2,chapter_title:"Components of Food",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the five major nutrients found in food?",back:"The five major nutrients are: (1) Carbohydrates \u2014 for energy, (2) Proteins \u2014 for growth and repair, (3) Fats \u2014 for reserve energy, (4) Vitamins \u2014 for protection, (5) Minerals \u2014 for structural functions like bone and blood formation.",memory_hook:"CPFVM \u2014 'Can Polar Foxes Visit Mongolia?' Carbohydrates, Proteins, Fats, Vitamins, Minerals."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the primary function of carbohydrates in the body?",back:"Carbohydrates are the body's primary and fastest source of energy. They are broken down into glucose, which fuels all body cells \u2014 especially the brain and muscles. Found in rice, wheat, potato, maize, and sugar.",memory_hook:"Carbs = Fuel. Like petrol in a car, carbohydrates are what the body burns first for instant energy."},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name three food sources of protein.",back:"Protein sources: dal (lentils), eggs, milk, meat (chicken, fish, mutton), soya bean, cheese, paneer, and nuts (groundnut, almonds). Proteins are the body's building material \u2014 used for growth, muscle repair, and making enzymes and antibodies.",memory_hook:"DEMS = Dal, Eggs, Milk, Soya \u2014 the four plant and animal protein champions. Add Nuts for bonus."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is roughage (dietary fibre) and why does our body need it?",back:"Roughage is the indigestible portion of plant food \u2014 mostly cellulose from the cell walls of plants. The body cannot digest it, but it is essential for keeping the digestive system healthy. It adds bulk to food, helps move food through the intestines, prevents constipation, and supports gut bacteria. Found in whole grains, vegetables, and fruits.",memory_hook:"Roughage = the broom of the digestive system. It sweeps food through and keeps the pipes clear."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a balanced diet?",back:"A balanced diet is one that contains all essential nutrients \u2014 carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, water, and roughage \u2014 in the correct proportions suitable for the person's age, sex, weight, and activity level. No single food provides all nutrients, so variety is key to a balanced diet.",memory_hook:"Balanced diet = all 7 components on one plate. Like a sports team \u2014 you need every position filled, not five strikers and no goalkeeper."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What colour does iodine turn when starch is present in a food sample?",back:"When iodine solution is added to a food containing starch, it turns blue-black. This is a positive result for the starch test. If no starch is present, iodine remains its original orange-brown colour (negative result). Foods that test positive: rice, potato, bread, wheat flour.",memory_hook:"Iodine + starch = Blue-Black. Think of navy blue ink staining a white shirt \u2014 dramatic, unmistakable colour change."},{id:"fc07",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you test for fat in a food sample?",back:"The paper test for fat: rub a small amount of the food on a piece of plain white paper. Hold the paper against light. A translucent (greasy, see-through) patch that does not dry away indicates fat is present. A wet patch that dries up is just water \u2014 not fat. Positive test foods: oil, butter, ghee, groundnut.",memory_hook:"Fat leaves a permanent see-through mark. Water patches dry. Oil patches stay. Like an oily fingerprint on paper \u2014 it never fully disappears."},{id:"fc08",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What reagents are used in the biuret test for protein, and what colour indicates a positive result?",back:"The biuret test for protein uses copper sulphate solution and caustic soda (sodium hydroxide) solution added to the food sample. A violet or purple colour indicates protein is present (positive result). If no protein is present, the mixture remains light blue. Positive test foods: egg white, milk, dal extract.",memory_hook:"Biuret = Blue to Purple. Copper sulphate is blue; protein turns it purple. B-to-P for Biuret-to-Protein."},{id:"fc09",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a deficiency disease? Give two examples.",back:"A deficiency disease is an illness caused by the prolonged lack of a specific nutrient in the diet. Examples: (1) Night blindness \u2014 caused by lack of Vitamin A; the person cannot see clearly in dim light. (2) Scurvy \u2014 caused by lack of Vitamin C; symptoms include bleeding gums and slow wound healing.",memory_hook:"Deficiency = Deficit (shortage). Each nutrient has its 'assigned disease' \u2014 miss it long enough and that disease appears."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Match each deficiency disease with the missing nutrient: Night blindness, Scurvy, Rickets, Anaemia, Goitre.",back:"Night blindness \u2192 Vitamin A deficiency. Scurvy \u2192 Vitamin C deficiency. Rickets \u2192 Vitamin D deficiency (children; soft, bent bones). Anaemia \u2192 Iron deficiency (low haemoglobin, fatigue, pale skin). Goitre \u2192 Iodine deficiency (swelling of thyroid gland in the neck).",memory_hook:"NSRAG in order \u2192 A, C, D, Iron, Iodine. 'Night Scurvy Ruins All Growth' = Night(A), Scurvy(C), Rickets(D), Anaemia(iron), Goitre(iodine)."},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are kwashiorkor and marasmus? What causes them?",back:"Both are severe protein deficiency diseases affecting children. Kwashiorkor: child has swollen belly (oedema), thin limbs, and discoloured hair \u2014 caused by adequate calorie intake but severe protein deficiency. Marasmus: child is severely underweight with visible ribs, no muscle or fat \u2014 caused by deficiency of both protein AND calories (overall starvation). Both are medical emergencies.",memory_hook:"K for Kwashiorkor = the child has a puffy tummy (K looks like a body with a round belly). M for Marasmus = Majorly thin, wasted \u2014 like a Marathon runner who ran out of all fuel."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do we need water as part of our daily diet, even though it has no calories?",back:"Water is essential for virtually every body function: it transports nutrients and oxygen through the blood, regulates body temperature through sweat, removes waste products through urine, enables chemical reactions in cells, lubricates joints, and aids digestion. The body is about 60\u201370% water. We can survive weeks without food but only days without water.",memory_hook:"Water = the body's courier, cooler, and cleaner. Without it, nothing works \u2014 no delivery, no cooling, no waste removal."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do fats provide more energy per gram than carbohydrates?",back:"Fats provide approximately 9 kilocalories per gram, while carbohydrates provide approximately 4 kilocalories per gram \u2014 making fats more than twice as energy-dense. This is because fats have more carbon-hydrogen bonds per molecule, and breaking these bonds releases more energy. This is why fat is an efficient long-term energy store.",memory_hook:"Fats = double-strength fuel. Same size tank of fat stores TWICE the energy as the same size tank of carbs."},{id:"fc14",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does Vitamin D deficiency cause rickets in children?",back:"Vitamin D is essential for the body to absorb calcium from food. Without Vitamin D, the body cannot properly absorb calcium from the diet, so bones remain weak and soft. In growing children, this causes rickets \u2014 the weight of the body bends the soft bones, leading to bow legs or knock knees. Vitamin D comes from sunlight exposure and foods like fish, eggs, and milk.",memory_hook:"Vit D = Doorman for calcium. Without the doorman (Vit D), calcium cannot enter the bones. No calcium in = soft bones = rickets."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A child in rural Karnataka is found to have difficulty seeing at night and dry, rough skin. Which nutrient is deficient, and which foods should be added to the diet?",back:"The symptoms (night blindness, dry skin) indicate Vitamin A deficiency. Foods rich in Vitamin A include: dark orange/yellow vegetables (carrot, pumpkin, sweet potato), dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, drumstick leaves, fenugreek), eggs, milk, and liver. Karnataka's NHM Vitamin A supplementation programme addresses this in children under 5.",memory_hook:"Orange = Vitamin A. Carrot, pumpkin, mango \u2014 all orange = rich in Vitamin A. Eyes see better in the dark with orange food."},{id:"fc16",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is eating whole wheat bread or brown rice healthier than white bread or white rice for digestion?",back:"Whole wheat bread and brown rice retain their outer bran layer (husk), which is rich in roughage (dietary fibre). White bread and white rice have had the bran removed during processing. The roughage in whole grains helps move food through the intestines, prevents constipation, and feeds healthy gut bacteria. Refined (white) grains provide carbohydrates but very little roughage.",memory_hook:"Brown = better for the bowel. The brown coat of whole grains = roughage. Remove the coat = remove the benefit."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which mineral prevents goitre, and what is the common way we get this mineral in our diet?",back:"Iodine is the mineral that prevents goitre. Iodine is needed by the thyroid gland (in the neck) to produce thyroid hormones. Deficiency causes the gland to enlarge, forming a visible swelling called goitre. The most common and effective way to prevent iodine deficiency is through iodised salt \u2014 common table salt with iodine added. This is now mandatory in India.",memory_hook:"Iodine \u2192 Iodised salt \u2192 no Goitre. The neck stays slim when the salt is right. Goitre = Gland gets too big without iodine."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"You test three foods with iodine: rice, apple, and boiled egg. Predict and explain the result for each.",back:"Rice \u2014 turns blue-black (positive starch test). Rice is a starchy food. Apple \u2014 remains orange-brown (negative starch test). Apple contains fruit sugar (fructose), not starch. Boiled egg \u2014 remains orange-brown (negative starch test). Egg contains protein and fat, not starch. Conclusion: iodine only tests for starch, not all carbohydrates or other nutrients.",memory_hook:"Starchy = blue-black. Sugary or protein-rich = no change. The iodine test is a starch detector, not a general food scanner."},{id:"fc19",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is no single food item enough for a healthy diet?",back:"No single food contains all the required nutrients in the right proportions. Rice provides carbohydrates but little protein or vitamins. Dal provides protein but little fat or calcium. Milk provides protein, fat, and calcium but little iron or Vitamin C. Only a variety of foods eaten together can supply all nutrients in adequate amounts \u2014 which is why a balanced diet requires combining multiple foods.",memory_hook:"Diet = team sport. Rice, dal, vegetables, milk, oil \u2014 each is a player with a different skill. The team wins only when all players are on the field."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Analyse: ragi mudde + sambar (dal, drumstick, tomato) + buttermilk. Which nutrients does this meal provide?",back:"Ragi mudde: carbohydrates + calcium (mineral). Sambar \u2014 toor dal: protein. Drumstick: Vitamin C, iron, Vitamin A. Tomato: Vitamin C. Spices (turmeric, pepper): trace minerals and antioxidants. Buttermilk (from milk): protein, calcium, probiotics. Total: carbohydrates, protein, vitamins (A, C), minerals (calcium, iron), and probiotics. A small addition of ghee or coconut would complete the fat component.",memory_hook:"Traditional Karnataka thali = accidental nutrition science. Generations of wisdom encoded in one meal."}]}},2897,[]);